INT. FEDERAL COURTHOUSE - HOLDING CORRIDOR - DAY

A narrow, windowless corridor behind the courtroom. Cinder block painted the color of old teeth. A bench bolted to the wall. At one end a steel door with a buzzer. At the other, a wall phone in a wire cage.

JUDGE PRUDENCE ADEKUNLE-WALSH, sixties, in her robe, stands in the doorway from chambers, tapping a gavel against her palm. DEFENSE COUNSEL THEODORE MBATHA, forties, paces. PROSECUTOR LUCINDA FAIRCLOUGH, thirties, sits on the bench with her briefcase on her knees. The defendant, MR. SEBASTIAN OKORO-LANE, fifties, sits cuffed on the opposite end, rocking. The court officer, DEPUTY MARCUS HOLLINGBERRY, forties, has a hand on his radio.
The wall phone rings. Everyone freezes. It rings again. Lucinda lifts her head. Theodore stops pacing. Sebastian stops rocking. Deputy Hollingberry unclips his radio and steps toward the phone.

HOLLINGBERRY
Hold on.

He lifts the receiver. A tinny voice crackles.

JUROR FOREPERSON (V.O.)
(over phone)
Your Honor? We've reached a verdict. But we have a problem.

HOLLINGBERRY
(to the group)
It's the jury. They've got a verdict.

JUDGE ADEKUNLE-WALSH
(to the deputy)
What problem?

HOLLINGBERRY
(into phone)
What problem?

JUROR FOREPERSON (V.O.)
(over phone)
One of us is missing. Number nine. He went to the restroom forty minutes ago.

The group erupts at once. Theodore swears. Lucinda stands, her briefcase sliding off her knees. The judge, unmoving, closes her eyes. Sebastian slumps to the floor and mutters at the tile.

SEBASTIAN
(to himself)
They lost a juror. They lost a juror.

THEODORE
(to the judge)
Your Honor, I move for a mistrial.

LUCINDA
(to the judge)
Your Honor, the state opposes. An alternate can be seated.

JUDGE ADEKUNLE-WALSH
(to everyone)
Quiet. All of you. Quiet.

The corridor falls silent. The judge walks the length of it, robe swishing, and takes the phone out of the deputy's hand. She listens for a moment.

JUDGE ADEKUNLE-WALSH (CONT'D)
(into phone)
Madam Foreperson, did anyone knock on the restroom door?

JUROR FOREPERSON (V.O.)
(over phone)
Yes, Your Honor.

JUDGE ADEKUNLE-WALSH
(into phone)
And?

JUROR FOREPERSON (V.O.)
(over phone)
He knocked back.

The judge hangs up. She hands the receiver to Hollingberry, turns to the corridor, and raises her gavel. Then she lowers it and, quietly, to nobody, says:

JUDGE ADEKUNLE-WALSH (CONT'D)
(muttering)
Thirty years on the bench.
